{"qid":"Q967","names":["Republic of Burundi"],"title":"Republic of Burundi","endonym":{"native":"Uburundi","lang":"rn"},"capsule":"Burundi is a small landlocked state in the African Great Lakes region whose modern republic emerged from the 1962 independence of the Belgian trust territory of Ruanda-Urundi, with the monarchy abolished by military coup in 1966. A recurring cycle of ethnic violence between Hutu and Tutsi populations — most catastrophically in the civil war of 1993–2005 following the assassination of President Melchior Ndadaye — has shaped its post-independence history. Peace was partially restored under the Arusha Agreement (2000) and subsequent elected governments, but President Pierre Nkurunziza's controversial third-term bid in 2015 triggered renewed crisis and international isolation. Burundi remains one of the world's poorest and most densely populated countries, dependent on subsistence agriculture and international aid.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern state boundaries are precisely defined by international treaty; the dataset's from-date of 1963 rather than the 1962 independence likely reflects a GIS source distinction or the 1966 abolition of the monarchy.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Burundi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burundi"},{"label":"Wikipedia: History of Burundi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Burundi"}],"britannica":"place/Burundi","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Burundi","rulers":[{"qid":"Q719817","name":"André Muhirwa","start":1961,"end":1963,"image":null},{"qid":"Q719834","name":"Pierre Ngendandumwe","start":1963,"end":1964